Based on my analysis, the new Postgres metadata plugin is a backend data-access layer that mirrors the existing Mongo plugin. The authorization/permission checks for metadata operations are performed at the higher-level core layer (lib/rucio/core/did.py) and the REST layer, not within the plugin itself — the Mongo reference plugin (mongo_meta.py) likewise performs no authorization checks inside its get/set/delete methods. The new plugin does not introduce a new authorization surface; it is invoked through the same `METADATA_PLUGIN_MODULES` dispatch in `__init__.py` which is subject to the same upstream permission checks. I could not locate an explicit policy rule in the retrieved documentation mandating per-plugin authorization, and the plugin's lack of internal auth checks is consistent with the established pattern. The SQL-injection concerns present in the code are not authorization flaws (CWE-639/862/863) and are out of scope for this review.

